Orthostatic Hypotension
Cardiovascular
Postural lightheadedness, Vision darkening on standing, Relief with sitting, BP drop >20/10 mmHg on standing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-031
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe dizziness and near-fainting when standing, resulting in a minor fall.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly patient experiencing a sudden fall preceded by profound dizziness when getting out of bed. While recovering quickly, urgent in-person physical assessment was required to exclude an acute arrhythmic or ischemic event, assess for traumatic injuries from the fall, and perform orthostatic vitals to confirm hemodynamic stability before the next routine clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I stand up, the room goes completely black, my legs feel like jelly, and I have to sit back down instantly before I pass out."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 48 hours, the patient reports significant lightheadedness and transient darkening of vision immediately upon standing from a seated or supine position. These symptoms are strictly related to postural changes. Patient describes a sensation of buckling in the legs and generalized weakness. Earlier today, this resulted in a minor, non-traumatic collapse back onto the bed. Notes immediate relief of symptoms upon sitting or lying down. Complains of a dull "coat-hanger" ache across the neck and shoulders after standing. KEY SYMPTOMS: Significant lightheadedness or dizziness immediately upon standing. Transient visual blurring or darkening of vision upon standing. Syncope or presyncope strictly related to postural changes. Relief of symptoms immediately upon sitting or lying down.
Functional Status: Currently restricted to bed/chair due to fear of falling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; high risk of traumatic injury from recurrent orthostatic falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP Supine: 135/80 | HR Supine: 72 | BP Standing (3 mins): 105/65 | HR Standing (3 mins): 88 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Frail appearing but alert, comfortable when supine, visibly pale and unsteady when asked to stand.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no primary cardiac structural abnormalities or murmurs. Peripheral pulses are normal volume when supine. Neurological: No focal motor or sensory deficits. No signs of head trauma from the reported minor fall. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Postural lightheadedness, Vision darkening on standing, Relief with sitting, BP drop >20/10 mmHg on standing (confirmed >30/15 mmHg drop on active stand testing).
Environmental Observations: Cluttered walkways and loose rugs observed, increasing fall risk.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently increased dose of Tamsulosin for benign prostatic hyperplasia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Orthostatic Hypotension (Likely iatrogenic) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strict reproducibility upon every postural change and the sustained, measurable drop in blood pressure without paradoxical bradycardia.

Cardiac Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symptoms occurring only upon standing, with immediate relief upon lying down, and a regular pulse without extra beats or deficits.

Vertigo (BPPV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sensation is described as "darkening vision and lightheadedness" (presyncope) rather than the room spinning (true vertigo), and is accompanied by a profound objective blood pressure drop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic symptoms of orthostatic hypotension, definitively confirmed by a massive >30/15 mmHg drop in blood pressure upon active stand testing. The recent increase in alpha-blocker therapy (Tamsulosin) points heavily to an iatrogenic cause. The resulting presyncopal episodes and recent near-fall pose a significant and immediate safety risk for an elderly patient living alone. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to diagnose the hemodynamic instability, adjust the offending medication, and implement immediate fall-prevention strategies, preventing a potentially catastrophic traumatic fall overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to withhold the Tamsulosin immediately. Recommended increased oral fluid intake (2 liters/day) and liberalized salt intake temporarily to expand intravascular volume.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to practice "counter-maneuvers" before walking: upon waking, sit on the edge of the bed for two full minutes, then pump both calf muscles vigorously 10 times before standing. By engaging the skeletal muscle pump, the patient actively forces venous blood back to the heart. The reward is a safe, dizzy-free transition to standing, reinforcing the new habit.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Advised on removing loose rugs and using a stable chair when dressing.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to review blood pressure trends and discuss alternative prostate management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences a complete loss of consciousness resulting in a hard fall, if they strike their head, or if they develop chest pain or shortness of breath, they must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on clinical orthostatic vitals; medication adjustments require ongoing GP supervision.
Clinical Course: Medication-induced blood pressure drops typically resolve within 24 to 48 hours of stopping the offending drug, provided hydration is maintained.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not stand up quickly. Always sit on the edge of the bed first. Call an ambulance if you fall and hit your head or cannot get up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your prostate medication is relaxing your blood vessels too much. When you stand up, gravity pulls the blood into your legs, and the vessels can't tighten fast enough to push it back to your brain, causing the dizziness. We need to stop this medication immediately.
Actionable Steps: Drink two large glasses of water now. Pump your legs before you stand. Use furniture for support when walking today.

